review_comments:
I don't like the solution, but I see a way we could avoid the SOC specific Kconfig definitions here without an index property- since the `CONTROLLER_ID` value is used at runtime, we could define a function to get the controller ID based on the base address of the controller.

Something like this:
```
/* Controller ID is for HAL usage */
/* LPC3511 IP */
#ifdef CONFIG_USB_DC_NXP_LPCIP3511
static usb_controller_index_t mcux_usb_controller_enum(void)
{
        return DT_INST_ENUM_HAS_VALUE(0, maximum_speed, high_speed) ?
                kUSB_ControllerLpcIp3511Hs0 :
                kUSB_ControllerLpcIp3511Fs0
}
#endif

/* EHCI IP */
#ifdef CONFIG_USB_DC_NXP_EHCI
static usb_controller_index_t mcux_usb_controller_enum(void)
{
/* This section is pulled from the MCUX SDK HAL driver for the ECHI, so the USBHS_BASE_ADDRS definition should
 * exist for any SOC with ECHI IP
 */
#if defined(USBHS_STACK_BASE_ADDRS)
        uint32_t base_addrs[] = USBHS_STACK_BASE_ADDRS;
#else
        uint32_t base_addrs[] = USBHS_BASE_ADDRS;
#endif
        return DT_INST_REG_ADDR(0) == base_addrs[0] ?
                kUSB_ControllerEhci0 :
                kUSB_ControllerEhci1;
}
#endif
```

We would then call `mcux_usb_controller_enum` in the `usb_dc_attach` function to get the controller ID value.

> It is a good idea but there is no guarantee that this pattern holds for future SOCs so I don't see the advantage of making this over-complicated

I expect this pattern should hold for new SOCs unless the HAL driver itself is modified. The code in the ECHI HAL driver for determining the base address to use for the USB IP looks like this:
```
#if defined(USBHS_STACK_BASE_ADDRS)
    uint32_t ehci_base[] = USBHS_STACK_BASE_ADDRS;
#else
    uint32_t ehci_base[] = USBHS_BASE_ADDRS;
#endif
....
ehciState->registerBase = (USBHS_Type *)ehci_base[controllerId - (uint8_t)kUSB_ControllerEhci0];
```

So we *should* always be able to determine the correct controller ID from the base address with something like this:

```
#if defined(USBHS_STACK_BASE_ADDRS)
        uint32_t base_addrs[] = USBHS_STACK_BASE_ADDRS;
#else
        uint32_t base_addrs[] = USBHS_BASE_ADDRS;
#endif
for (uint8_t i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(base_addrs); i++) {
	if (base_addrs[i] == DT_INST_REG_ADDR(0)) {
		return kUSB_ControllerEhci0 + i;
}
```

> And sometimes the HAL puts a random 0 in the first index of USB_BASE_ADDRS which would break things. 

If you look at the RT1052, which does this- the `USBHS_STACK_BASE_ADDRS` macro is also defined with the correct array for the ECHI driver. So I'm not sure this would be an issue, since we'd just use the `USBHS_STACK_BASE_ADDRS` definition.

The core thing I want to avoid here is a growing ladder of `#ifdef` blocks for each SOC NXP enables with this driver. Ideally a developer should be able to determine the type of IP block an SOC is using for USB, and the driver should compile and work with it.

> If we really want a better solution it has to come from the SDK itself is my opinion. Either that or make the driver zephyr native as the usb_dc_kinetis is, but both of those things are outside the scope of this PR which is just a quick fix of the abuse of devicetree.

This is true- at the end of the day a lot of the challenges in this driver come from the SDK interface
